hi there steve kaufman here um  my wife is playing the piano in the  background it's a rainy  sunday here in vancouver a little bit of  musical accompaniment  today i'm going to talk about learning  and my belief that learning  has to come before studying  in other words it's very difficult to  study something  that you haven't already learned  naturally in some way and this is  particularly true  of languages  and unfortunately most of our  educational situations involve  an attempt to teach  or an attempt to study something  which we haven't already become familiar  with  i remember once  driving home in tokyo in the 70s  listening to nhk radio they had a  program or they had an educational  channel  and i heard this sound coming from my  radio in my car and it went something  like this  and this went on and on  and i thought it was some kind of  buddhist chant  and then i found out that this was their  radio english lesson  i don't know what they were trying to  achieve  uh i remember buying a korean cd and i  still remember that they began with all  the sounds of korean  uh which you had to listen to and repeat  you didn't know what they meant  and i still remember these sounds all  new paul pew i can't i don't know what  it means i never came across those  sounds or words again but that's  what they had there for you  um  you know and uh  when you like i can remember two  starting into my russian a lot of the  books will give you a whole list of  cognates words that are  similar to other languages  two pages three pages worth what can i  possibly do with those words i can't  possibly learn them  however  if i come across them  and discover them i'll be very happy to  find a freebie  word in russian that i already know from  some other language but i can't possibly  take them all at once until i have had  that exposure to russian the same with  grammar rules you know in russian for  example one rule that came at me fairly  early was that for numbers if you are if  it's one year then it's in the year is  in the nominativ if it's two three or  four it's in the genitive singular but  if it's five or more it's in the  genitive plural  and  that's one as in 21 31 41 it doesn't  matter  um  yeah okay you know kind of understand  what you're saying there but if that and  if that was the only rule i had to learn  in russian  you know i might  be able to pick up on that when you  consider that there are so many rules  rules about  cases and you know the same ending can  be  the genitive the accusative the plural  nominative depending on gender and  whatever  uh  you know sometimes the case it's the ac  it's in the it's the uh it'll be this  ending if it's  animate like animal or person or some  other ending if it's not animal  the verb to go depending on whether  you're going on foot on by train or by  car is different depending on whether  you're going and coming back or you're  just going  you know all of these things that are  very difficult to understand  particularly when you have no reference  point  none  uh  now that i've been studying russian for  three years  these things are starting to seem  natural to me i can now go back to a  grammar book  and read this thing and it starts to  make sense  the same is true  with um  with sounds you know you have all these  rules  that  you know and they'll even try and teach  you this  you know when such a letter comes next  to some other letter the one will  influence the other and it's kind of  pronounced differently  so uh there you know i can't remember  all these rules  but and then they introduced the  phonetic alphabet stuff but if you've  never heard the word before what's the  phonetic alphabet going to do for you  um so  my big thing is you can focus on  studying something on refining things  once you've got the language in you  there was a comment  on my you know let the words overflow  video where i said the main thing is  just to let the words come at you learn  some forget some get some get some  familiarity with the language and work  on gaining these words  uh and don't worry about what you forget  and so someone commented on my youtube  video here well yes but i think you have  to work on grammar and words grammar and  vocabulary at the same time  nice theory  i can't do it  if you give me two or three things about  the language like  verb comes at the end  a couple of things like that i might be  able to remember them but very quickly  it's  overload it's tilt i can't make head or  tail of it  until i have had a lot of exposure to  the language  i had a similar comment from someone  on where i had said you know in terms of  accent reduction you know don't  deliberately focus on that especially  not at the beginning  just  listen and if you want to project  yourself  into that culture that language and  imitate that person  you'll be able to do it slowly you'll  get better and better at it  and this person sort of said well you  know you really should work on your  accents so i said well why would you say  that and they came back and said well  because when you spoke portuguese you  didn't sound very good you had strong  spanish  uh influence on your portuguese well  first of all i have never claimed  to be native-like in any of the  languages that i speak it's not my goal  i have never tried to do that  i try to imitate the native speaker but  i don't pretend  that my goal  is to achieve  such a degree of  accentless whatever liners that people  would mistake me for a native speaker  they will not however while i speak it i  will betray somewhere that i'm not a  native speaker  and languages that i'm not working on  that hard like portuguese  of course i'm gonna have a spanish  accent people tell me i have a french  accent in my german  uh  so what  uh i mean if i were an actor  and i had a role where i had to pretend  that i spoke  portuguese like a brazilian or like a  portuguese then that's very specific  then i would work at it i might get a  special coach to coach me on my accent  but if my main goal is to enjoy the  language  communicate  enjoy myself i'm not going to worry  about those things  so i guess what i'm saying here is  that a lot of sort of deliberate  studying deliberate instruction  is almost counterproductive  that there is this  sufist saying that you can't  learn what you don't already know i  would put it a different way i would say  that you can't deliberately study  something that you haven't already  learned or experienced  um  i think the brain works that way things  come through the brain kind of picks up  on it a little bit it goes in there  somewhere in the head then you get at it  again and you get it like i'll often if  i start a new language before we had  link i would go out and buy  teach yourself  korean colloquial korean somebody else's  pimsleur korean assimil korean not  pimple because he's got too much english  on there but asimil  and so then i would listen to the same  stuff from three or four different  sources so again i'm just passing it  through the hippocampus if you want  passing it through my brain it settles  in there somewhere then very similar  content comes at me from another  direction and slowly slowly i'm sort of  layering  stuff in there that's going to stick and  once i have enough of that then i might  want to go in and study because there  will be things that i don't notice there  will be things that can be pointed out  in a grammar book can be pointed out by  sort of saying well you know really you  may have not have noticed but  uh  in whatever language they tend to drop  this vowel before this constant and so  you can see stuff there that oh yeah  right i kind of wondered about that or  or even i never noticed and so at that  point these little things can come along  and help you but they are just  supplementary to the main task of  learning which i believe is done before  you actually start studying so my advice  is before yo